@article{lenz2025macura,

\paragraph{Value Overestimation.}
\citet{thrun1993issues} first identified maximization bias in Q-learning with function approximation. Double Q-learning \citep{vanhasselt2016double} decouples action selection from evaluation; TD3 \citep{fujimoto2018td3} extends this to actor-critic methods via clipped double-Q critics; REDQ \citep{chen2021redq} further mitigates overestimation through randomized Q-function ensembles. These model-free solutions target the max operator as the amplifier. In model-based settings, MBPO \citep{janner2019mbpo} bounds model use via short rollouts and MOPO \citep{yu2020mopo} penalizes rewards by model uncertainty. \citet{lu2018nondelusional} identify delusional bias from policy-class constraints. Our CIVO is mechanistically distinct: the amplifier is not the max operator or policy constraints, but the world model's co-adaptation with a learned temporal embedding, producing systematic reward-prediction bias at $\dt > 1$. \citet{tallec2019making} provide theoretical grounding by showing Q-function degradation with temporal resolution mismatches.

\paragraph{Model Fidelity and Objective Mismatch.}
\citet{chua2018pets} demonstrate that probabilistic ensembles can quantify dynamics-model uncertainty for planning, but this uncertainty is consumed by the planner rather than bootstrapped through a critic. \citet{lambert2020objective} formalize the \emph{objective mismatch} problem: minimizing observation-prediction loss does not guarantee reward-prediction accuracy. \citet{ma2024harmonydream} address this in DreamerV3 by harmonizing reconstruction and reward loss weighting. Our gradient decomposition (\S\ref{sec:gradient_decomposition}) provides direct evidence: 93\% of temporal embedding gradients originate from observation decoders while 0\% come from the reward head, confirming that observation reconstruction dominates embedding learning. \citet{palenicek2025diminishing} show diminishing returns of value expansion with increasing rollout length, consistent with our finding that $\dt > 1$ imagination compounds model errors without proportional benefit. \citet{frauenknecht2025rollouts} demonstrate rollout-quality dominance in MBRL; we identify the specific $\dt$-embedding-induced mechanism by which rollout divergence corrupts values.

\paragraph{Network Capacity and Plasticity.}
\citet{lyle2022capacity} demonstrate progressive capacity loss in deep RL, where early-learned features resist overwriting. \citet{nikishin2023plasticity} propose plasticity injection to counteract this degradation. We invoke capacity loss to explain why the same CIVO trigger produces divergent outcomes across seeds (\S\ref{sec:seed_divergence}): if the critic's capacity is partially consumed at CIVO onset, the network cannot recalibrate; if sufficient capacity remains, transient recovery is possible. Our factorial diagnostic approach follows \citet{engstrom2020implementation} and \citet{andrychowicz2021whatmatters}.

